8286: # Decides whether a pending record (from Read-PendingFile) still belongs to a live
8287: # consultation. Returns { Active; Message; Check }. The rules, in this order:
8288: #   1. THE WRITER (0.4.x wave 21, D1) decides first: a record that names the bridge that
8289: #      wrote it (pid + start_time) is ACTIVE while that process runs on this host - in EVERY
8290: #      state, `reserved` included (a panel member building its prompt, running its reviewer,
8291: #      committing; a panel run whose members have not started yet). Never by pid alone (a
8292: #      record without start_time - older bridges - skips this rule), never this very
8293: #      process, not across hosts.
8294: #   2. When the writer is gone or unknown, the state decides:
8295: #      reserved            inactive: nothing was started under it;
8296: #      running / survivors / committing
8297: #                          active while child_pid (with its start time) or any survivor pid
8298: #                          is alive on this host; pids from another host: active (they
8299: #                          cannot be checked from here); all recorded pids gone: the
8300: #                          process scan below (a dead launcher is no proof of a dead tree);
8301: #      launching           the child may or may not exist: the process scan below; from
8302: #                          another host (no pids to check): treated as dead.
8303: #   3. The process scan: children of the recorded writer and of the recorded pids (Windows
8304: #      keeps an orphan's parent id), then - for a record OUTSIDE a panel only - the
8305: #      machine-wide "looks like codex" rule (Find-CodexProcesses; "task not verifiable").
8306: #      A panel member's record (`panel`) NEVER uses that name rule: a live sibling member's
8307: #      reviewer looks like codex too (F03-2, F04-5); outside Windows, where orphans are
8308: #      reparented, it is judged by its recorded pids + start times only.
